-- A permission whose exercise creates somebody else's obligation: the shape in
-- which a lapse and a HENCE part company.
--
-- `the option` is an option to purchase. If the buyer exercises it, the seller
-- owes a transfer. If the buyer does not, the option LAPSES and nobody owes
-- anything — the rule ends fulfilled. Those are two different destinations, and
-- the deadline is what decides between them.
--
-- Until 2026-09-17 the state graph drew only one of them. L4.StateGraph emitted
-- no LEST edge for a single-party MAY, so the graph had no route to FULFILLED
-- at all, and L4.Bpmn.Lower filled the gap by synthesising a timer of its own
-- and sending it "wherever HENCE lands" — which here is the seller's
-- obligation. The emitted diagram therefore said that a buyer who lets the
-- option expire obliges the seller to transfer the shares anyway.
--
-- It went unnoticed because every other permission in this corpus has no HENCE,
-- or a HENCE of FULFILLED, and in those shapes the two arms land on the same
-- state and the guess is invisible (see `handover`, whose four permissions all
-- route to the same Fulfilled end event either way). This file is the witness
-- for the shape where the guess is wrong.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Buyer  HAS name IS A STRING
    Seller HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Exercise HAS who IS AN Actor
    Transfer HAS who IS AN Actor, to IS AN Actor

theBuyer  MEANS Buyer OF "Ms Tan"
theSeller MEANS Seller OF "Mr Lim"

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`the option` MEANS
    PARTY  theBuyer
    MAY    Exercise (EXACTLY theBuyer)
    WITHIN 30
    HENCE  (PARTY  theSeller
                MUST   Transfer (EXACTLY theSeller) (EXACTLY theBuyer)
                WITHIN 14)
